{"caller": "codex", "commit": "e6293cfce0221ff87af8192143a59e753f04f529", "date": "2026-10-06T14:59:40+00:00", "exit": 0, "files": ["conformance/corpus_shards.py", "conformance/native_binding_validation.py", "conformance/native_bindings.py", "conformance/native_call_recording.py", "conformance/native_record.py", "conformance/native_statements.py", "conformance/upstream_helpers.py", "plans/20261006-exact-tcl-values.status.md", "reviews/log.jsonl", "tests/conformance_tcl_binding_policy_test.py", "tests/conformance_tcl_precision_test.py"], "findings": [{"id": "20261006T145940Z-e6293cfc#1", "line": 121, "path": "conformance/native_call_recording.py", "rule": "R10", "severity": "should", "text": "`check_source_results` now runs outside the damaged-evidence wrapper. It reads `setupOutcomes`, `setupResults`, `setupErrors` and `trace[-1][\"primaryCode\"]` (in `upstream_fidelity.check_results`), but `validate_fields` never checks those fields. A damaged corpus record now raises a bare `KeyError`, `IndexError` or `TypeError` that does not name the case or give a recovery action, and the `try` removed at `corpus_shards.py:82` used to catch exactly these errors. A length mismatch in those fields (the `ValueError` from `zip(..., strict=True)`) also gets the wrong label: \"Native fidelity difference ... Exclude this source assertion\" instead of the recapture advice. Fix: wrap `KeyError`, `TypeError` and `IndexError` from `check_source_results` as damaged binding evidence with the recapture message (or check those fields in `validate_fields` first), and add a test where a Tcl record is missing `setupResults`."}], "id": "20261006T145940Z-e6293cfc", "kind": "review", "reviewer": "claude", "revision": "@-", "rules": {"R1": "62d446279093", "R10": "638d8cbd1df8", "R11": "5de19cdbbbc1", "R12": "99f66579f467", "R13": "da0926dafb88", "R2": "e5e78fb2a52a", "R3": "4f9188eadd60", "R4": "ea7ca17539eb", "R5": "b8613a2d693b", "R6": "5600dce59aef", "R7": "0b68df581c57", "R8": "b4168359c60d", "R9": "5a69196344d0"}, "well_formed": true}
